    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d470">

                <pb facs="0621" n="609" sameAs="frus:frus1946v07/pg609"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">867N.01/5–1946: Circular telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State
                        to Certain Diplomatic and Consular Officers
                    </hi><anchor n="44" xml:id="d470-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                        <lb/>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">us urgentn   niact
                        </hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1946-05-19T08:00:00-04:00">May 19, 1946—8
                            a.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>On May 20 you should address communication to Govt to which you are accredited
                    enclosing text of memo which is being transmitted in Dept’s immediately
                    following telegram as well as copy of report of Anglo-American Committee of
                    Inquiry with appendices. Memo is that referred to in Dept’s circular May
                        16<anchor n="45" xml:id="d470-f2"/> and is also being sent together with
                    copy of report to interested groups in this country. British representatives in
                    your area will probably receive instructions from their Govt also to ascertain
                    views of Arabs and Jews and you should offer to associate yourself with them in
                    approach. Cairo will approach Arab League and Govt of Yemen as well as Egyptian
                    Govt and Jerusalem will approach Arab Higher Committee, Jewish Agency and Govt
                    of Trans-Jordan. Dept plans make public in near <pb facs="0622" n="610"
                        xml:id="pg610"/> future fact that views of Arabs and Jews have been
                        requested<anchor n="46" xml:id="d470-f3"/> and will release text of
                    memo.</p>
                <p>Beirut inform Damascus.</p>
                <p>Repeated London for information only.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Acheson</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d470-f1" n="44" type="source" xml:id="d470-f1-content">
                <p>At Beirut, Cairo, Baghdad, Jidda, and Jerusalem. The circular telegram was also
                    repeated to London with the following addition: “Memo mentioned is same as
                    contained in Dept’s 4136 of May 19, noon.” For No. 4136, see <hi
                        rendition="#italic">infra</hi>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d470-f2" n="45" xml:id="d470-f2-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d470-f3" n="46" xml:id="d470-f3-content">
                <p>For text of the Department’s announcement on May 21 that “the Government of the
                    United States, in conjunction with the British Government, has taken steps to
                    initiate the consultations with Jewish and Arab leaders regarding Palestine to
                    which both Governments have been committed”, see Department of State <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Bulletin</hi>, June 2, 1946, p. 956. The text of the
                    Department’s letter of May 20 seeking the views of various American
                    organizations with a particular interest in Palestine is printed <hi
                        rendition="#italic">ibid.</hi>
                </p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
